1. Linear convolution to remove the wrapping-around effects. 
   The current implementation of convolution is circular, causing the late signals to wrap 
   around to the beginning of the seismograms. To fix this issue, zero-padding can be 
   employed. However, applying zero-padding to time-domain seismograms requires 
   computationally expensive steps:
     a. Reading all the DSM FFT data.
     b. Performing an inverse FFT (IFFT).
     c. Adding zeros to the time-domain seismograms.
     d. Recomputing the FFT.
   While doubling the signal length with zero-padding offers a simple solution for even-index 
   FFT values (which remain identical to the original FFT), odd-index FFT values require sinc 
   interpolation using the original FFT values (zero-padding in the time domain
   corresponds to sinc interpolation in the frequency domain). Then we need to read all the DSM
   frequencies in for interpolation, which is computationally expensive and memory-size demanding.
